WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.370
Start with Monday, January ninth,

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:04.250
twenty twelve.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.890
At seven in the morning, issue a
forecast for the hour beginning at eight.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:14.803
The already fitted hour and working day
mean returns three hundred and forty nine

00:00:14.803 --> 00:00:17.530
point five zero rentals per hour.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.050
The recorded outcome later turns
out to be four hundred and twenty.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.710
That answer was not part
of the original request.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:30.350
The prediction is too low, but we
need both its direction and its size.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.180
This review keeps the earlier fitted
model unchanged and studies its saved

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:35.730
predictions.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.570
No new linear regression is fitted.

00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:44.863
Our scoring residual is
prediction minus observed target.

00:00:45.433 --> 00:00:48.779
Subtract four hundred and twenty from
three hundred and forty nine point five

00:00:48.779 --> 00:00:49.303
zero.

00:00:50.113 --> 00:00:53.903
The result is negative seventy
point five zero rentals per hour.

00:00:54.813 --> 00:00:58.043
A negative sign means
underprediction with this convention.

00:00:58.813 --> 00:01:02.063
The absolute error is positive
seventy point five zero.

00:01:02.953 --> 00:01:07.283
A boosting correction used target
minus prediction, the opposite sign,

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:10.643
because it described a move
the model still needed to make.

00:01:11.113 --> 00:01:15.963
Here we are measuring a fixed forecast,
not taking another optimization step.

00:01:16.413 --> 00:01:20.213
Naming the convention prevents the same
number being interpreted in opposite

00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:21.023
directions.

00:01:23.393 --> 00:01:27.463
Change the requested hour to two
in the morning on the same Monday.

00:01:28.013 --> 00:01:33.443
The corresponding group predicts seven
point five one against a target of five,

00:01:33.533 --> 00:01:35.843
giving positive two point five one.

00:01:36.473 --> 00:01:38.003
This is an overestimate.

00:01:38.673 --> 00:01:43.857
Adding the two absolute errors and
dividing by two gives thirty six point

00:01:43.857 --> 00:01:44.803
five one.

00:01:45.393 --> 00:01:50.469
Adding the signed errors instead gives a
mean near negative thirty three point nine

00:01:50.469 --> 00:01:51.143
nine.

00:01:51.813 --> 00:01:55.503
Overprediction can cancel
underprediction in the signed mean,

00:01:55.713 --> 00:01:58.823
while both contribute
to mean absolute error.

00:01:59.473 --> 00:02:01.743
These two hours explain the arithmetic.

00:02:02.313 --> 00:02:05.403
They do not replace the
complete validation population.

00:02:07.858 --> 00:02:10.928
The reader first compares record identity,

00:02:11.558 --> 00:02:12.488
target time,

00:02:12.938 --> 00:02:17.048
issue time and row order with
the frozen prepared rows.

00:02:17.698 --> 00:02:22.638
It also verifies the saved target and
checks that each exported error column

00:02:22.638 --> 00:02:25.428
agrees with prediction minus target.

00:02:25.938 --> 00:02:30.608
Only after this alignment do we
reduce residuals into summary numbers.

00:02:31.358 --> 00:02:36.078
One integration check deliberately
reorders a prediction file and updates its

00:02:36.078 --> 00:02:36.828
checksum.

00:02:37.058 --> 00:02:39.768
The identity check still rejects it.

00:02:40.358 --> 00:02:44.308
A matching checksum alone cannot
establish correct target alignment.

00:02:45.378 --> 00:02:48.768
The shown excerpt combines those
identity fields with the arithmetic;

00:02:49.438 --> 00:02:53.428
the complete source retains the
validation branches and specific errors.

00:02:55.525 --> 00:02:57.825
Across validation, three thousand,

00:02:57.825 --> 00:03:01.435
one hundred and forty three
hours are underpredicted.

00:03:01.845 --> 00:03:06.025
Their deficits total about three hundred
and fifty seven thousand and eighty nine

00:03:06.025 --> 00:03:08.475
rentals across one hour records.

00:03:09.205 --> 00:03:10.838
Divide by all four thousand,

00:03:10.838 --> 00:03:16.495
three hundred and fifty seven observations
to obtain eighty one point nine six.

00:03:17.145 --> 00:03:21.898
Divide only by underpredicted observations
to obtain one hundred and thirteen point

00:03:21.898 --> 00:03:22.935
six one.

00:03:23.725 --> 00:03:28.305
Those summaries answer different questions
about average burden and the typical

00:03:28.305 --> 00:03:29.835
deficit when one occurs.

00:03:30.465 --> 00:03:33.695
Neither measures unmet
demand or station shortages.

00:03:34.465 --> 00:03:38.965
The dataset records observed rentals
and does not establish an inventory or

00:03:38.965 --> 00:03:40.255
financial consequence.

00:03:42.442 --> 00:03:47.392
The complete validation mean absolute
error is ninety one point five seven,

00:03:47.762 --> 00:03:52.052
with root mean squared error one hundred
and thirty seven point three nine.

00:03:52.742 --> 00:03:56.632
Squaring before averaging gives
large misses more influence.

00:03:57.122 --> 00:04:00.632
Taking the square root returns
the result to rental units.

00:04:01.502 --> 00:04:05.502
Mean signed error is negative
seventy two point three four,

00:04:05.502 --> 00:04:07.532
showing overall underprediction.

00:04:08.602 --> 00:04:13.152
The largest retained miss occurs on March
twenty third at five in the afternoon:

00:04:13.602 --> 00:04:17.237
about three hundred and ninety five
predicted against nine hundred and fifty

00:04:17.237 --> 00:04:18.412
seven observed.

00:04:19.362 --> 00:04:21.152
We retain that difficult hour.

00:04:21.522 --> 00:04:25.132
Explaining a failure does not
authorize removing it from the score.

